{'title': {'value': 'Reproducibility study of "Robust Fair Clustering: A Novel Fairness Attack and Defense Framework"'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'This reproducibility study examines "Robust Fair Clustering: A Novel Fairness Attack and Defense Framework" by Chhabra et al. (2023), an innovative work in fair clustering algorithms. Our study focuses on validating the original paper's claims concerning the susceptibility of state-of-the-art fair clustering models to adversarial attacks and the efficacy of the proposed Consensus Fair Clustering (CFC) defence mechanism. We employ a similar experimental framework but extend our investigations by using additional datasets. Our findings confirm the original paper's claims, reinforcing the vulnerability of fair clustering models to adversarial attacks and the robustness of the CFC mechanism.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Lucas Ponticelli', 'Vincent Loos', 'Eren Kocadag', 'Kacper Bartosik']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Lucas_Ponticelli1', '~Vincent_Loos1', '~Eren_Kocadag1', '~Kacper_Bartosik1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/c46420585946803fa469fd0a007cb1296ad9201a.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': "@article{
ponticelli2024reproducibility,
title={Reproducibility study of ''Robust Fair Clustering: A Novel Fairness Attack and Defense Framework''},
author={Lucas Ponticelli and Vincent Loos and Eren Kocadag and Kacper Bartosik},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Xu1sEPhjqH},
note={Reproducibility Certification}
}"}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Jonathan_Scarlett1'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': '- Reviewer 1Q4j pointed out an error in Figures 1 through 4 where not all metrics where equal when 0% of the samples were poisoned. This error was caused by a mistake in the code for plotting, where we labelled the x-axis from 0 through 30%, but entered the data from 12.5 through 30%. We have fixed this and included the datapoints at 0%. 
- Reviewer 1Q4j also noticed that the pre-attack metrics were varying for the CFC algorithm, while they should be constant. This mistake was caused by the fact that the codebase from the original paper did not apply random seeds for the CFC algorithm, causing variation when running the pre-attack with different percentages of poisoned data. This is also reflected in the Figure 4 of the original paper (Chhabra et al, 2023). We overlooked this problem, and simply re-used their code. However, we have now fixed it by simply taking the mean of all values for the pre-attack for each metric.
- Fixed a typo that said "standard variation" instead of "standard deviation".
- Introduced more clarity for the hyperparameter selection, as requested by Action Editor Dw6m.'}, 'code': {'value': 'https://github.com/vinloo/robust-fair-clustering-reproducibility'}, 'certifications': {'value': ['Reproducibility Certification']}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'ponticelli|reproducibility_study_of_robust_fair_clustering_a_novel_fairness_attack_and_defense_framework'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Solving the Tree Containment Problem Using Graph Neural Networks'}, 'abstract': {'value': '\textsc{Tree containment} is a fundamental problem in phylogenetics useful for verifying a proposed phylogenetic network, representing the evolutionary history of certain species. \textsc{Tree containment} asks whether the given phylogenetic tree (for instance, constructed from a DNA fragment showing tree-like evolution) is contained in the given phylogenetic network. In the general case, this is an NP-complete problem. We propose to solve it approximately using Graph Neural Networks. In particular, we propose to combine the given network and the tree and apply a Graph Neural Network to this network-tree graph. This way, we achieve the capability of solving the tree containment instances representing a larger number of species than the instances contained in the training dataset (i.e., our algorithm has the inductive learning ability). Our algorithm demonstrates an accuracy of over $95\%$ in solving the tree containment problem on instances with up to 100 leaves.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Arkadiy Dushatskiy', 'Esther Julien', 'Leen Stougie', 'Leo van Iersel']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Arkadiy_Dushatskiy1', '~Esther_Julien1', '~Leen_Stougie1', '~Leo_van_Iersel1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/2cae2fee56057224368dd578fde1b53906c6d3fa.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
