}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Fredrik_Daniel_Johansson1'}, 'code': {'value': 'https://github.com/BorealisAI/dcf'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'liu|dynaconf_dynamic_forecasting_of_nonstationary_time_series'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Enhancing Low-Precision Sampling via Stochastic Gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'Low-precision training has emerged as a promising low-cost technique to enhance the training efficiency of deep neural networks without sacrificing much accuracy. Its Bayesian counterpart can further provide uncertainty quantification and improved generalization accuracy.
This paper investigates low-precision sampling via Stochastic Gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (SGHMC) with low-precision and full-precision gradient accumulators for both strongly log-concave and non-log-concave distributions. Theoretically, our results show that to achieve $\epsilon$-error in the 2-Wasserstein distance for non-log-concave distributions, low-precision SGHMC achieves quadratic improvement ($\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left({\epsilon^{-2}{\mu^*}^{-2}\log^2\left({\epsilon^{-1}}\right)}\right)$) compared to the state-of-the-art low-precision sampler, Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics (SGLD) ($\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left({{\epsilon}^{-4}{\lambda^{*}}^{-1}\log^5\left({\epsilon^{-1}}\right)}\right)$). Moreover, we prove that low-precision SGHMC is more robust to the quantization error compared to low-precision SGLD due to the robustness of the momentum-based update w.r.t. gradient noise. 
Empirically, we conduct experiments on synthetic data, and MNIST, CIFAR-10 \& CIFAR-100 datasets, which validate our theoretical findings. Our study highlights the potential of low-precision SGHMC as an efficient and accurate sampling method for large-scale and resource-limited machine learning.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Ziyi Wang', 'Yujie Chen', 'Qifan Song', 'Ruqi Zhang']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Ziyi_Wang9', '~Yujie_Chen2', '~Qifan_Song1', '~Ruqi_Zhang1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/185cbdacd8c079af1129858cd16f657735c90d57.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'supplementary_material': {'value': '/attachment/722e96f6696b5c3ff61fa669cd3dd5aeadd8ed81.zip'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
wang2024enhancing,
title={Enhancing Low-Precision Sampling via Stochastic Gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo},
author={Ziyi Wang and Yujie Chen and Qifan Song and Ruqi Zhang},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=uSLNzzuiDJ},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Ruoyu_Sun1'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'Camera Ready Revision'}, 'code': {'value': 'https://github.com/comeusr/Low-precisionSGHMC'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'wang|enhancing_lowprecision_sampling_via_stochastic_gradient_hamiltonian_monte_carlo'}}

{'title': {'value': 'CoDeC: Communication-Efficient Decentralized Continual Learning'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'Training at the edge utilizes continuously evolving data generated at different locations. Privacy concerns prohibit the co-location of this spatially as well as temporally distributed data, deeming it crucial to design training algorithms that enable efficient continual learning
over decentralized private data. Decentralized learning allows serverless training with spatially distributed data. A fundamental barrier in such setups is the high bandwidth cost of communicating model updates between agents. Moreover, existing works under this training paradigm are not inherently suitable for learning a temporal sequence of tasks while retaining the previously acquired knowledge. In this work, we propose CoDeC, a novel communication-efficient decentralized continual learning algorithm that addresses these challenges. We mitigate catastrophic forgetting while learning a distributed task sequence by incorporating orthogonal gradient projection within a gossip-based decentralized learning algorithm. Further, CoDeC includes a novel lossless communication compression scheme based on the gradient subspaces. We theoretically analyze the convergence rate for our algorithm and demonstrate through an extensive set of experiments that CoDeC successfully learns distributed continual tasks with minimal forgetting. The proposed compression scheme results in up to 4.8× reduction in communication costs without any loss in performance.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Sakshi Choudhary', 'Sai Aparna Aketi', 'Gobinda Saha', 'Kaushik Roy']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Sakshi_Choudhary1', '~Sai_Aparna_Aketi1', '~Gobinda_Saha1', '~Kaushik_Roy1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/be61c8a25bb81682fec82b0318a964fe60156cb4.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
choudhary2024codec,
title={CoDeC: Communication-Efficient Decentralized Continual Learning},
author={Sakshi Choudhary and Sai Aparna Aketi and Gobinda Saha and Kaushik Roy},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=N05OnQG1BA},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Cedric_Archambeau1'}, 'video': {'value': 'https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UPp5btj8j_JziRPeyWbEB6f7y_KBFsZY/view?usp=sharing'}, 'code': {'value': 'https://github.com/Sakshi09Ch/CoDeC'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'choudhary|codec_communicationefficient_decentralized_continual_learning'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Statistical Component Separation for Targeted Signal Recovery in Noisy Mixtures'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'Separating signals from an additive mixture may be an unnecessarily hard problem when one is only interested in specific properties of a given signal. In this work, we tackle simpler "statistical component separation" problems that focus on recovering a predefined set of statistical descriptors of a target signal from a noisy mixture. Assuming access to samples of the noise process, we investigate a method devised to match the statistics of the solution candidate corrupted by noise samples with those of the observed mixture. We first analyze the behavior of this method using simple examples with analytically tractable calculations. Then, we apply it in an image denoising context employing 1) wavelet-based descriptors, 2) ConvNet-based descriptors on astrophysics and ImageNet data. In the case of 1), we show that our method better recovers the descriptors of the target data than a standard denoising method in most situations. Additionally, despite not constructed for this purpose, it performs surprisingly well in terms of peak signal-to-noise ratio on full signal reconstruction. In comparison, representation 2) appears less suitable for image denoising. Finally, we extend this method by introducing a diffusive stepwise algorithm which gives a new perspective to the initial method and leads to promising results for image denoising under specific circumstances.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Bruno Régaldo-Saint Blancard', 'Michael Eickenberg']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Bruno_Régaldo-Saint_Blancard1', '~Michael_Eickenberg5']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/160965f4f15ec842fb4f7f05b471e444f9e00134.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': "@article{
blancard2024statistical,
title={Statistical Component Separation for Targeted Signal Recovery in Noisy Mixtures},
author={Bruno R{\'e}galdo-Saint Blancard and Michael Eickenberg},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=OUWG6O4yo9},
note={}
}"}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Stephen_Becker1'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'Camera ready version incorporating revisions from the reviewing process.'}, 'code': {'value': 'https://github.com/bregaldo/stat_comp_sep'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'blancard|statistical_component_separation_for_targeted_signal_recovery_in_noisy_mixtures'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Provable Membership Inference Privacy'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'In applications involving sensitive data, such as finance and healthcare, the necessity for preserving data privacy can be a significant barrier to machine learning model development. Differential privacy (DP) has emerged as one canonical standard for provable privacy. However, DP’s strong theoretical guarantees often come at the cost of a large drop in its utility for machine learning; and DP guarantees themselves are difficult to interpret. In this work, we propose a novel privacy notion, membership inference privacy (MIP), as a step towards addressing these challenges. We give a precise characterization of the relationship between MIP and DP, and show that in some cases, MIP can be achieved using less amount of randomness compared to the amount required for guaranteeing DP, leading to smaller drop in utility. MIP guarantees are also easily interpretable in terms of the success rate of membership inference attacks in a simple random subsampling setting. As a proof of concept, we also provide a simple algorithm for guaranteeing MIP without needing to guarantee DP.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Zachary Izzo', 'Jinsung Yoon', 'Sercan O Arik', 'James Zou']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Zachary_Izzo1', '~Jinsung_Yoon1', '~Sercan_O_Arik1', '~James_Zou1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/ef41ebb611e141873182b83651071e806f27b4f6.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
