journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=WeiRR8h87X},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Novi_Quadrianto1'}, 'code': {'value': 'https://github.com/pouyamghari/OFMS-FT'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'ghari|budgeted_online_model_selection_and_finetuning_via_federated_learning'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Empowering GNNs via Edge-Aware Weisfeiler-Leman Algorithm'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'Message passing graph neural networks (GNNs) are known to have their expressiveness upper-bounded by 1-dimensional Weisfeiler-Leman (1-WL) algorithm. To achieve more powerful GNNs, existing attempts either require \emph{ad hoc} features, or involve operations that incur high time and space complexities. In this work, we propose a \textit{general} and \textit{provably powerful} GNN framework that preserves the \textit{scalability} of the message passing scheme. In particular, we first propose to empower 1-WL for graph isomorphism test by considering edges among neighbors, giving rise to NC-1-WL. The expressiveness of NC-1-WL is shown to be strictly above 1-WL and below 3-WL theoretically. Further, we propose the NC-GNN framework as a differentiable neural version of NC-1-WL. Our simple implementation of NC-GNN is provably as powerful as NC-1-WL. Experiments demonstrate that our NC-GNN performs effectively and efficiently on various benchmarks.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Meng Liu', 'Haiyang Yu', 'Shuiwang Ji']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Meng_Liu3', '~Haiyang_Yu6', '~Shuiwang_Ji1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/3a825cb5fc7d829acd960c3180b83e4d3acd9b4c.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
liu2024empowering,
title={Empowering {GNN}s via Edge-Aware Weisfeiler-Leman Algorithm},
author={Meng Liu and Haiyang Yu and Shuiwang Ji},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=VDy6LgErFM},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Yaoliang_Yu1'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'liu|empowering_gnns_via_edgeaware_weisfeilerleman_algorithm'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Separability Analysis for Causal Discovery in Mixture of DAGs'}, 'abstract': {'value': "Directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) are effective for compactly representing causal systems and specifying the causal relationships among the system's constituents. Specifying such causal relationships in some systems requires a mixture of multiple DAGs -- a single DAG is insufficient. Some examples include time-varying causal systems or aggregated subgroups of a population. Recovering the causal structure of the systems represented by single DAGs is investigated extensively, but it remains mainly open for the systems represented by a mixture of DAGs. A major difference between single- versus mixture-DAG recovery is the existence of node pairs that are separable in the individual DAGs but become inseparable in their mixture. This paper provides the theoretical foundations for analyzing such inseparable node pairs. Specifically, the notion of \emph{emergent edges} is introduced to represent such inseparable pairs that do not exist in the single DAGs but emerge in their mixtures. Necessary conditions for identifying the emergent edges are established. Operationally, these conditions serve as sufficient conditions for separating a pair of nodes in the mixture of DAGs. These results are further extended, and matching necessary and sufficient conditions for identifying the emergent edges in tree-structured DAGs are established. Finally, a novel graphical representation is formalized to specify these conditions, and an algorithm is provided for inferring the learnable causal relations."}, 'authors': {'value': ['Burak Varici', 'Dmitriy Katz', 'Dennis Wei', 'Prasanna Sattigeri', 'Ali Tajer']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Burak_Varici1', '~Dmitriy_A_Katz1', '~Dennis_Wei1', '~Prasanna_Sattigeri1', '~Ali_Tajer1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/8bce103ee6264402f2e569c6c7fc7ec3f1074c5f.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'supplementary_material': {'value': '/attachment/e134d7c9837e9d1f5943e479c2e51e4ad921e162.zip'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
varici2024separability,
title={Separability Analysis for Causal Discovery in Mixture of {DAG}s},
author={Burak Varici and Dmitriy Katz and Dennis Wei and Prasanna Sattigeri and Ali Tajer},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
