{'title': {'value': 'Towards Fair Video Summarization'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'Automated video summarization is a vision task that aims to generate concise summaries of lengthy videos. Recent advancements in deep learning have led to highly performant video summarization models; however, there has been a lack of attention given to fairness and unbiased representation in the generated summaries. To bridge this gap, we introduce and analytically define the fair video summarization problem, and demonstrate its connections to the well-established problem of fair clustering. To facilitate fair model development, we also introduce the FairVidSum dataset, which is similar in design to state-of-the-art video summarization datasets such as TVSum and SumMe, but also includes annotations for sensitive attributes and individuals alongside frame importance scores. Finally, we propose the SumBal metric for quantifying the fairness of an outputted video summary. We conduct extensive experiments to benchmark the fairness of various state-of-the-art video summarization models. Our results highlight the need for better models that balance accuracy and fairness to ensure equitable representation and inclusion in summaries. For completeness, we also provide a novel fair-only baseline, FVS-LP, to showcase the fairness-utility gap models can improve upon.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Anshuman Chhabra', 'Kartik Patwari', 'Chandana Kuntala', 'Sristi', 'Deepak Kumar Sharma', 'Prasant Mohapatra']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Anshuman_Chhabra1', '~Kartik_Patwari1', '~Chandana_Kuntala1', '~Sristi1', '~Deepak_Kumar_Sharma1', '~Prasant_Mohapatra1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/65074c117966e339963b7d3f1248c52e6d5b7973.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
