}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Yujia_Li1'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'We have revised the submission to address feedback received from the reviewers and action editor.'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'gabrielsson|rewiring_with_positional_encodings_for_graph_neural_networks'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Variational Classification: A Probabilistic Generalization of the Softmax Classifier'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'We present a latent variable model for classification that provides a novel probabilistic interpretation of neural network softmax classifiers. We derive a variational objective to train the model, analogous to the evidence lower bound (ELBO) used to train variational auto-encoders, that generalises the cross-entropy loss used to train classification models. Treating inputs to the softmax layer as samples of a latent variable, our abstracted perspective reveals a potential inconsistency between their anticipated distribution, required for accurate label predictions to be output, and the empirical distribution found in practice. We augment the variational objective to mitigate such inconsistency and encourage a chosen latent distribution, instead of the implicit assumption in off-the-shelf softmax classifiers. Overall, we provide new theoretical insight into the inner workings of widely-used softmax classification. Empirical evaluation on image and text classification datasets demonstrates that our proposed approach, variational classification,  maintains classification accuracy while the reshaped latent space improves other desirable properties of a classifier, such as calibration, adversarial robustness, robustness to distribution shift and sample efficiency useful in low data settings.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Shehzaad Zuzar Dhuliawala', 'Mrinmaya Sachan', 'Carl Allen']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Shehzaad_Zuzar_Dhuliawala3', '~Mrinmaya_Sachan3', '~Carl_Allen1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/3976158a5b4631ec2fde036da66549f31580b93d.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'supplementary_material': {'value': '/attachment/26216301cb3a653eda63f74bb763603c7edae337.zip'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
dhuliawala2024variational,
title={Variational Classification: A Probabilistic Generalization of the Softmax Classifier},
author={Shehzaad Zuzar Dhuliawala and Mrinmaya Sachan and Carl Allen},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2024},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=EWv9XGOpB3},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Frederic_Sala1'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'Minor cosmetic changes post-acceptance, e.g. adding author names and github link.

Added "A Probabilistic Generalization of the Softmax Classifier" to title to differentiate from a few other recent uses of the term "Variational Classifier" that have come to light.'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'dhuliawala|variational_classification_a_probabilistic_generalization_of_the_softmax_classifier'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Synthetic Data from Diffusion Models Improves ImageNet Classification'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'Deep generative models are becoming increasingly powerful, now generating diverse, high fidelity, photo-realistic samples given text prompts.  Nevertheless, samples from such models have not been shown to significantly improve model training for challenging and well-studied discriminative tasks like ImageNet classification. In this paper we show that augmenting the ImageNet training set with samples from a generative diffusion model can yield substantial improvements in ImageNet classification accuracy over strong ResNet and Vision Transformer baselines. To this end we explore the fine-tuning of large-scale text-to-image diffusion models, yielding class-conditional ImageNet  models with state-of-the-art FID score (1.76 at 256×256 resolution) and Inception Score (239 at 256×256). The model also yields a new state-of-the-art in Classification Accuracy Scores, i.e., ImageNet  test accuracy for a ResNet-50 architecture trained solely on synthetic data (64.96 top-1 accuracy for 256×256 samples, improving to 69.24 for 1024×1024 samples). Adding up to three times as many synthetic samples as real training samples consistently improves ImageNet classification accuracy across multiple architectures.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Shekoofeh Azizi', 'Simon Kornblith', 'Chitwan Saharia', 'Mohammad Norouzi', 'David J. Fleet']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Shekoofeh_Azizi1', '~Simon_Kornblith1', '~Chitwan_Saharia1', '~Mohammad_Norouzi1', '~David_J._Fleet1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/7cdfe447a8b633fedc7c9c890d7a9198d689aea4.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'supplementary_material': {'value': '/attachment/890a7afe7ca6e886cbe570569f69a0056f7a564b.pdf'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'N/A'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
