journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2023},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=9pWjgQ3y85},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Murat_A_Erdogdu1'}, 'supplementary_material': {'value': '/attachment/28c1d95d5c02ef21661f5b96b959051d6045bb4a.pdf'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'Please see the supplementary file for a diff version of the PDF'}, 'certifications': {'value': []}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'domingoenrich|an_explicit_expansion_of_the_kullbackleibler_divergence_along_its_fisherrao_gradient_flow'}}

{'title': {'value': 'The Geometry of Mixability'}, 'abstract': {'value': "Mixable loss functions are of fundamental importance in the context of prediction with expert advice in the online setting since they characterize fast learning rates. By re-interpreting properness from the point of view of differential geometry, we provide a simple geometric characterization of mixability for the binary and multi-class cases: a proper loss function $\ell$ is $\eta$-mixable if and only if the superprediction set $\textrm{spr}(\eta \ell)$ of the scaled loss function $\eta \ell$ slides freely inside the superprediction set $\textrm{spr}(\ell_{\log})$ of the log loss $\ell_{\log}$, under fairly general assumptions on the differentiability of $\ell$. Our approach provides a way to treat some concepts concerning loss functions (like properness) in a ''coordinate-free'' manner and reconciles previous results obtained for mixable loss functions for the binary and the multi-class cases."}, 'authors': {'value': ['Armando J Cabrera Pacheco', 'Robert Williamson']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Armando_J_Cabrera_Pacheco1', '~Robert_Williamson1']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/a1001ebec4c73811d5db6f068c5e2c703678a45e.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
cabrera pacheco2023the,
title={The Geometry of Mixability},
author={Armando J Cabrera Pacheco and Robert Williamson},
journal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},
issn={2835-8856},
year={2023},
url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=VrvGHDSzZ7},
note={}
}'}, 'assigned_action_editor': {'value': '~Daniel_M_Roy1'}, 'changes_since_last_submission': {'value': 'Corrected typos. Incorporation of some additional content/comments suggested by the reviewers.'}, 'license': {'value': 'Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)'}, 'paperhash': {'value': 'pacheco|the_geometry_of_mixability'}}

{'title': {'value': 'Expected Worst Case Regret via Stochastic Sequential Covering'}, 'abstract': {'value': 'We study the problem of sequential prediction and online minimax regret with stochastically generated features under a general loss function. In an online learning setting, Nature selects features and associates a true label with these features. A learner uses features to predict a label, which is compared to the true label, and a loss is incurred. The total loss over $T$ rounds, when compared to a loss incurred by a set of experts, is known as a regret. We introduce the notion of *expected worst case minimax regret* that generalizes and encompasses prior known minimax regrets. For such minimax regrets, we establish tight upper bounds via a novel concept of *stochastic global sequential covering*. We show that for a hypothesis class of VC-dimension $\mathsf{VC}$ and $i.i.d.$ generated features over $T$ rounds, the cardinality of stochastic global sequential covering can be upper bounded with high probability (w.h.p.) by $e^{O(\mathsf{VC} \cdot \log^2 T)}$. We then improve this bound by introducing a new complexity measure called the *Star-Littlestone* dimension, and show that classes with Star-Littlestone dimension $\mathsf{SL}$ admit a stochastic global sequential covering of order $e^{O(\mathsf{SL} \cdot \log T)}$. We further establish upper bounds for real valued classes with  finite fat-shattering numbers. Finally, by applying information-theoretic tools for the fixed design minimax regrets, we provide lower bounds for  expected worst case minimax regret.  We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach by establishing tight bounds on the expected worst case minimax regrets for logarithmic loss and 
general mixable losses.'}, 'authors': {'value': ['Changlong Wu', 'Mohsen Heidari', 'Ananth Grama', 'Wojciech Szpankowski']}, 'authorids': {'value': ['~Changlong_Wu1', '~Mohsen_Heidari1', '~Ananth_Grama1', '~Wojciech_Szpankowski2']}, 'pdf': {'value': '/pdf/f7ef88799c8a98c47d6c499447caf846a73b0788.pdf'}, 'submission_length': {'value': 'Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)'}, 'venue': {'value': 'Accepted by TMLR'}, 'venueid': {'value': 'TMLR'}, '_bibtex': {'value': '@article{
wu2023expected,
title={Expected Worst Case Regret via Stochastic Sequential Covering},
author={Changlong Wu and Mohsen Heidari and Ananth Grama and Wojciech Szpankowski},
